\subsection{Comparison of Pipeline A (Bowtie2) and Pipeline B (Standalone Python/\texttt{edlib})}

Two methodological patterns emerge when comparing Pipeline~A and Pipeline~B:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Threshold sensitivity on E19:} Under Pipeline~B, E19 on-target classification decreases monotonically with stringency: {{e19_pb_m1_on_pct}}\% (M1) $\rightarrow$ {{e19_pb_m2_75_on_pct}}\% (\texttt{edlib} $\ge 75\%$) $\rightarrow$ {{e19_pb_m2_80_on_pct}}\% (\texttt{edlib} $\ge 80\%$) $\rightarrow$ {{e19_pb_m2_85_on_pct}}\% (\texttt{edlib} $\ge 85\%$), while ungapped matching yields {{e19_pb_ungap75_on_pct}}\%. At $\ge 80\%$, Pipeline~B agrees with Pipeline~A ({{e19_pa_ontarget_pct}}\%) within 0.01~pp at the aggregate level, though their retained read populations differ ({{e19_pb_classified}} vs. {{e19_pa_classified}} classified pairs).
  \item \textbf{Divergence on PS2 and PS3:} On PS2 and PS3, Pipeline~B assigns 3.2--4.4~pp fewer on-target reads than Pipeline~A (PS2: {{ps2_pb_m2_75_on_pct}}\% vs. {{ps2_pa_ontarget_pct}}\%; PS3: {{ps3_pb_m2_75_on_pct}}\% vs. {{ps3_pa_ontarget_pct}}\%) and slightly higher dimer fractions ({{ps2_pb_dimer_pct}}\% vs. {{ps2_pa_dimer_pct}}\%; {{ps3_pb_dimer_pct}}\% vs. {{ps3_pa_dimer_pct}}\%). Pipeline~A aligns long reads with Bowtie2 \emph{before} applying the 8-bp $5'$ primer prefix hash, retaining amplicon reads with minor $5'$ errors, whereas Pipeline~B filters {{ps2_pb_unmatched_pct_raw}}\% and {{ps3_pb_unmatched_pct_raw}}\% of raw reads as primer-unmatched. Retaining open-source Bowtie2 therefore tracks the original procedure more closely.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Cell-by-Cell Matrix Validation (PS1--PS3) and Heavy-Tailed Concentration (E19)}

To move beyond aggregate percentages, we compared all six reconstructed 96-plex dimer pair tables (archived under \path{data/matrices/} with directional columns \texttt{reads\_p1\_p2} and \texttt{reads\_p2\_p1} alongside unordered totals \texttt{dimer\_reads}, preserving the full ordered $192 \times 192$ matrix and 18,528 unordered pairs) cell-by-cell against both conditions in Supplementary Data~3 (\texttt{MOESM7}; Table~\ref{tab:matrix}, Figure~\ref{fig:dimer}B). Because Pearson correlation can be dominated by high-count pairs and all-cell exact agreement is inflated by structural zeros, Table~\ref{tab:matrix} reports Spearman $\rho$, exact agreement restricted to the active-pair union (\textbf{Act.\ Exact}), active-pair Jaccard overlap, MAE, and normalized L1 total absolute error ($\sum |r_i - p_i| / \sum p_i$).

