\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Against the \texttt{conc optimized} matrices, Pipeline~A achieves Pearson $r = {{pa_conc_opt_r6_min}}$--${{pa_conc_opt_r6_max}}$, normalized L1 error of {{pa_conc_opt_l1_min}}--{{pa_conc_opt_l1_max}}\%, active-pair Jaccard overlap of {{pa_conc_opt_jacc_min}}--{{pa_conc_opt_jacc_max}}, exact integer agreement on {{pa_conc_opt_exact_act_min}}--{{pa_conc_opt_exact_act_max}}\% of active-union cells ({{pa_conc_opt_exact_all_min}}--{{pa_conc_opt_exact_all_max}}\% across all 18,528 cells including shared zeros), total count ratios of {{pa_conc_opt_ratio_min}}--{{pa_conc_opt_ratio_max}}, and 10/10 overlap among the top-10 pairs (Pipeline~B achieves Pearson $r = {{ps1_pb_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}$, ${{ps2_pb_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}$, and ${{ps3_pb_mv_conc_optimized_r5}}$). Direct count stratification against \texttt{MOESM7} shows that {{pa_conc_opt_low12_cell_min}}--{{pa_conc_opt_low12_cell_max}}\% of disagreeing cells have $\max(\text{rec},\text{pub}) \le 2$ reads (contributing {{pa_conc_opt_low12_l1_min}}--{{pa_conc_opt_low12_l1_max}}\% of total L1 reads), whereas high-count cells ($\ge 10$ reads) contribute {{ps1_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ge10_l1_pct}}\% (PS1), {{ps2_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ge10_l1_pct}}\% (PS2), and {{ps3_pa_mv_conc_optimized_ge10_l1_pct}}\% (PS3) of total L1 count differences despite small fractional offsets. By contrast, correlations with the \texttt{not optimized} matrices are far lower ($r = {{pa_not_opt_r_min}}$--${{pa_not_opt_r_max}}$ across PS1--PS3), resolving an undocumented provenance detail: the Figshare \texttt{E14} FASTQs correspond to the concentration-optimized PS1--PS3 libraries.

In the 384-plex E19 library, dimer formation across the {{e19_pb_total_pairs}} candidate unordered pairs is highly sparse and heavy-tailed (Figure~\ref{fig:dimer}A; no formal power-law fit is claimed). Under Pipeline~B, {{e19_pb_zero_pairs}} pairs ({{e19_pb_zero_pct}}\%) yield zero dimer reads, {{e19_pb_active_pairs}} pairs ({{e19_pb_active_pct}}\%) exhibit $\ge 1$ read, and {{e19_pb_severe_pairs}} pairs ({{e19_pb_severe_pct}}\%) exhibit $\ge 10$ reads. A single reverse--reverse pair (\texttt{{{e19_pb_top1_p1}}} $\times$ \texttt{{{e19_pb_top1_p2}}}) accounts for {{e19_pb_top1_reads}} of {{e19_pb_dimer_n}} dimer reads (\textbf{{{e19_pb_top1_pct}}\%}; top-10 pairs: {{e19_pb_top10_pct}}\%). Under Pipeline~A, the same dominant pair accounts for {{e19_pa_top1_reads}} of {{e19_pa_dimer_n}} dimer reads (\textbf{{{e19_pa_dimer_top1_pct}}\%}) across {{e19_pa_dimer_active}} active pairs. Although low-count active pair totals differ between pipelines ({{e19_pa_dimer_active}} vs. {{e19_pb_active_pairs}}), the dominant interaction and strong concentration of dimer reads are robust across both pipelines.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig2_dimer_concentration_and_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Pair-level primer-dimer structure and matrix validation. \textbf{(A)} Log-log rank-abundance curve of the {{e19_pb_active_pairs}} active dimer pairs in E19 (generated from archived \texttt{data/saddle\_384\_dimer\_pairs.tsv}), showing heavy-tailed concentration in rank-1 pair \texttt{{{e19_pb_top1_p1}}} $\times$ \texttt{{{e19_pb_top1_p2}}} ({{e19_pb_top1_pct}}\%). \textbf{(B)} Cell-by-cell Pearson correlation across all 18,528 unordered 96-plex pairs against published \texttt{MOESM7} sheets, verifying strong matrix agreement ($r \ge {{pa_conc_opt_r6_min}}$ for Pipeline~A) and identifying the Figshare E14 files as the concentration-optimized libraries.}
\label{fig:dimer}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Matched-Pair Uniform Random Audits of Alignment Scope and Indel Recovery}

To avoid tile-order bias and cross-denominator comparisons, we drew a seeded uniform reservoir sample across all {{audit_pop_nondimer}} qualifying Pipeline~B non-dimer read pairs in E19 (\texttt{seed={{audit_seed}}}), evaluated \emph{all three alignment methods on the exact same $N = {{c75_n}}$ read pairs}, and archived complete per-read classifications in \path{data/matched_pair_audit_5000.tsv.gz}:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Matched-pair alignment comparison ($N = {{c75_n}}$ identical non-dimer pairs):}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{Paired-end Bowtie2 (on the exact same {{c75_n}} pairs):} Both mates map to the 384-amplicon reference for \textbf{{{bm_bt2_pct}}\%} of pairs ({{bm_bt2_n}}/{{c75_n}}; 95\% Wilson CI: {{bm_bt2_ci}}\%), identical to the proper-pair concordant set ({{bm_bt2_conc_n}}/{{c75_n}}, {{bm_bt2_pct}}\%). Exactly {{bm_ge60_n}} pairs meet the $\ge 60$~bp length criterion on both mates ({{bm_bt2_ge60_pct}}\% mapped under $\ge 60$~bp; {{bm_lt60_n}} pairs have at least one mate $<60$~bp).
    \item \emph{Cognate-anchored vs. all-384-target \texttt{edlib} ($\ge 75\%$):} Cognate-anchored = \textbf{{{c75_cog_on_pct}}\%} (95\% CI: {{c75_cog_on_ci}}\%) vs. dual-strand all-target = \textbf{{{c75_all_on_pct}}\%} (95\% CI: {{c75_all_on_ci}}\%); concordance = {{c75_conc_pct}}\% ({{c75_conc_ci}}\%), with {{c75_cog_off_all_on}} non-cognate reads matching a target (all individually auditable in \path{data/matched_pair_audit_5000.tsv.gz}). Among cognate-labelled reads, {{c75_cog_cross_best}}/{{c75_cog_tot}} strictly better and {{c75_cog_tied_best}}/{{c75_cog_tot}} tied non-cognate best matches passed the 75\% threshold; across all similarity levels, {{audit_strict_below75_n}} cognate-labelled reads exhibited slightly higher non-cognate alignments below both acceptance thresholds ({{audit_strict_below75_alt_range}}\% vs. {{audit_strict_below75_cog_range}}\%). Against matched paired-end Bowtie2, cognate \texttt{edlib} $\ge 75\%$ achieves {{bm_c75_conc}}\% concordance ({{bm_c75_conc_ci}}\%, $\Delta = {{bm_c75_diff}}$~pp) and all-target \texttt{edlib} $\ge 75\%$ achieves {{bm_a75_conc}}\% ({{bm_a75_conc_ci}}\%, $\Delta = {{bm_a75_diff}}$~pp).
    \item \emph{Cognate-anchored vs. all-384-target \texttt{edlib} ($\ge 80\%$):} Cognate-anchored = \textbf{{{c80_cog_on_pct}}\%} (95\% CI: {{c80_cog_on_ci}}\%) vs. all-target = \textbf{{{c80_all_on_pct}}\%} (95\% CI: {{c80_all_on_ci}}\%); concordance = {{c80_conc_pct}}\% ({{c80_conc_ci}}\%; {{c80_cog_cross_best}} strictly better and {{c80_cog_tied_best}} tied non-cognate best matches passing 80\%). Against matched Bowtie2, cognate \texttt{edlib} $\ge 80\%$ achieves {{bm_c80_conc}}\% concordance ({{bm_c80_conc_ci}}\%, $\Delta = {{bm_c80_diff}}$~pp) and all-target \texttt{edlib} $\ge 80\%$ achieves {{bm_a80_conc}}\% ({{bm_a80_conc_ci}}\%, $\Delta = {{bm_a80_diff}}$~pp).
  \end{itemize}
  Single-end \texttt{edlib} classified a larger fraction of the matched read pairs as on-target than paired-end Bowtie2. These differences may reflect differences in mate requirements, alignment scoring, target search, and acceptance thresholds.
  \item \textbf{Gapped vs. ungapped indel recovery ($N = {{ind_n}}$ random cognate reads from {{audit_pop_cognate}}):} Exactly {{ind_rec_n}} reads ({{ind_rec_pct}}\%, 95\% CI: {{ind_rec_ci}}\%) passed gapped \texttt{edlib} ($\ge 75\%$) after failing ungapped positional matching ($\ge 75\%$). Full CIGAR parsing showed that \textbf{{{ind_gaps_pct}}\%} ({{ind_gaps_n}}/{{ind_rec_n}}) contained alignment gaps ({{ind_subonly_n}} substitution-only), with {{ind_le2_n}} ({{ind_le2_pct}}\%) having $\le 2$~bp indels and {{ind_gt2_n}} ({{ind_gt2_pct}}\%) having $>2$~bp or multi-gap events (median {{ind_med_bp}}~bp, IQR {{ind_iqr_lo}}--{{ind_iqr_hi}}~bp). This documents alignment gaps under our computational procedure without attributing them to biological insertions or deletions.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Limitations, Verification, and Availability}

Three scope limitations apply. First, 21--30\% of raw sequencing pairs are excluded during adapter trimming, quality filtering, and primer matching, and Pipelines~A and~B retain slightly different read populations (28-bp vs. 17-bp adapter rules and Bowtie2-first vs. primer-first routing); aggregate differences between the two pipelines therefore reflect both alignment logic and retained-read differences. Second, SADDLE's simulated annealing primer-design algorithm is not publicly released and is not replicated here. Third, we did not execute the original scripts inside commercial MATLAB, so minor boundary differences against MATLAB \texttt{swalign} cannot be excluded.

To verify end-to-end portability beyond the original Windows workstation, we performed an author-conducted clean-room reproduction in a fresh Kaggle Cloud Linux container (\texttt{Linux 6.18.55+}) using an isolated environment created from \path{environment.yml} (Python 3.12.4, Bowtie2 2.5.5, \texttt{edlib} 1.3.9.post1, Matplotlib 3.10.9; distinct from third-party verification by an independent researcher). All 13 external inputs were downloaded and checksum-verified, all 10 unit tests passed, both scientific JSON registries (0 diffs) and all 9 decompressed pair/audit tables matched byte-for-byte, and regenerated PNG figures passed declared cross-platform visual tolerances (\texttt{EXIT\_CODE=0}; transcripts archived alongside WSL2 Linux logs in \path{data/verification_logs/}).

In conclusion, our open-source Python + Bowtie2 reimplementation reproduces SADDLE's 96-plex dimer suppression ({{ps1_pa_dimer_pct}}\% $\rightarrow$ {{ps2_pa_dimer_pct}}\% $\rightarrow$ {{ps3_pa_dimer_pct}}\% vs. {{ps1_pub_dimer}}\% $\rightarrow$ {{ps2_pub_dimer}}\% $\rightarrow$ {{ps3_pub_dimer}}\%), 384-plex E19 read partitions ({{e19_pa_ontarget_pct}}\% / {{e19_pa_nonspec_pct}}\% / {{e19_pa_dimer_pct}}\% vs. $\sim${{e19_pub_ontarget}}\% / $\sim${{e19_pub_nonspec}}\% / $\sim${{e19_pub_dimer_int}}\%), and published 96-plex dimer matrices ($r = {{pa_conc_opt_r6_min}}$--${{pa_conc_opt_r6_max}}$, normalized L1 error {{pa_conc_opt_l1_min}}--{{pa_conc_opt_l1_max}}\%) without commercial MATLAB. All code (MIT License), pinned environments (\path{environment.yml}, \texttt{requirements-\allowbreak lock-\allowbreak py312.txt}, \path{requirements.txt}), automated checksum-verified source downloader (\path{code/download_raw_data.py}), archived directional and unordered pair tables, per-read matched audit evidence, clean-room Kaggle and WSL2 verification logs (\path{data/verification_logs/}), synchronized LaTeX and Markdown manuscripts, and an automated verification suite (\path{code/verify_replication.py}, supporting isolated raw-FASTQ recomputation via \texttt{--full}) are packaged for release; raw sequencing data remain hosted on Figshare under CC~BY~4.0\supercite{xie2021figshare}.

